      <groundspeak:encoded_hints>BCP 29CM BGL 2.4M W C/L N-S TR

BRASS CAP 29 CM BGL. 36.6M NE OF THE NE COR OF THE LARGEST RED BLDG IN THE AREA; 2.4M W OF THE C/L OF A N-S TRAIL; AND 14.6M N OF A 1.8M MKR POST</groundspeak:encoded_hints>
      <groundspeak:logs>
        <groundspeak:log id="654481840">
          <groundspeak:date>2016-12-04T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Didn't find it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="2319799">CanadianRockies</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP577 - Burstall

Posted: N50° 39.618 W109° 57.119&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The crops were harvested, but the frozen ground didn't yield any cap for us today. No obvious holes in the ground, and a few random pokes of the screwdriver didn't produce anything (which wasn't surprising if the cap really is nearly a foot below ground level).&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="517853012">
          <groundspeak:date>2015-07-06T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="70898">Tommi Potx</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;SitRep Op Sandpiper, Day 3.

BCP577 Burstall

 A short drive due west of Burstall is CFB Suffield [aka Suffer-field] Canadas' largest military training area., and home of Defense Research Establishment Suffield [DRES]. 

Heres an event for you: A nuke sim, designed to test ventilation systems in hardened buildings,  the 500-Ton event called DIAL PACK was conducted on 23 July 1970 at the Defence Research Establishment Suffield (DRES)- an isolated, 1000 square mile area in Alberta, Canada consisting entirely of barren prairie. It was a completely spherical ball of 32.6 pound blocks of cast TNT. The sphere was nearly 27 feet in diameter or almost as tall as a four story building. The lower third was encased in styrofoam to support it. 

That and a lot more weird, strange and deadly military stuff goes on that can quite literally, &quot;take your breath away&quot;.... [like nerve gas... &lt;img src="/images/icons/icon_smile_sad.gif" border="0" align="middle" /&gt;]

Never volunteer for temporary duty at DRES!!

TFTC!!&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="332739523">
          <groundspeak:date>2013-07-22T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Didn't find it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="836983">Coolcat21</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP577 – Burstall&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When I turned on to the field road about 70 m from this cap's promised ground zero, I was faced with the classic Saskatchewan question: should I go with wheat or canola: which will really pay off in the end? The cap hint suggested that the cap was west of the road so canola it was. I slipped and slid down the wet, muddy road to see if I could make it my cinderella crop. &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But it was not to be. As mentioned in past logs, there's no marker post anymore so there was no help there. My ground zero put me smack dab in the middle of the road so I concentrated on an area just west of that amongst the waist and belly high canola plants. During my 45 minute metal detecting sessions I got only one  beep and in about 3 minutes I was the proud owner of an old iron spike. I shall cherish it always.  &lt;img src="/images/icons/icon_smile.gif" border="0" align="middle" /&gt; &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I wouldn't say I was strictly thorough about covering the ground in a logical way. Canola is a tough, sinewy plant and it was challenging to actually get the detector  head through it in anywhere near a consistent way. I'd say the time to search for this one would be early spring before whatever crop is in; or late fall after the crop's been taken off. Then you'll just be dealing with stubble.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Not all was for naught though. On the way out I found a perfectly good set of pump pliers on the ground near where I parked. Bonus!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks for luring me briefly over the line to my native province!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This entry was edited by Coolcat21 on Sunday, 28 July 2013 at 02:29:15 UTC.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="240114044">
          <groundspeak:date>2012-06-04T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Didn't find it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="664594">Sears Tower</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP577 - Burstall&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Oh how I wanted to find this one.  After driving an hour out of our way and searching until it was really dark, we had no luck.  We did find a half dozen or so spike with the metal detector.  We dug many holes, but could not find the goods.  Maybe next time.  The marker post is gone and the area is now tilled.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
